WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.880
So, there's some folks out there in

00:00:02.159 --> 00:00:07.279
school or in corporate America or in any

00:00:04.880 --> 00:00:09.679
club or business environment and I'm

00:00:07.279 --> 00:00:12.480
about to give them some, frankly, some

00:00:09.679 --> 00:00:14.320
cold hard truth about networking. I want

00:00:12.480 --> 00:00:15.839
to tell them how I feel about networking

00:00:14.320 --> 00:00:18.320
and I want to shift their mindset on

00:00:15.839 --> 00:00:21.039
networking. When I was in college, I

00:00:18.320 --> 00:00:22.560
went to networking events and I didn't

00:00:21.039 --> 00:00:24.480
realize this till later, but every

00:00:22.560 --> 00:00:25.920
single person at that networking event,

00:00:24.480 --> 00:00:27.519
they walked around with their hand out

00:00:25.920 --> 00:00:29.199
saying, "Help me. Help me. Help me. Help

00:00:27.519 --> 00:00:30.800
me. Help me. Help me. Help me. Help me.

00:00:29.199 --> 00:00:32.239
help me. Shaking hands. Hey, how can you

00:00:30.800 --> 00:00:33.920
help me? Hey, hey, hey, how can you help

00:00:32.239 --> 00:00:36.239
me? Oh, oh, by the way, can you help me?

00:00:33.920 --> 00:00:40.480
Can you help me? Can you help me? That's

00:00:36.239 --> 00:00:43.040
how 90 95% of people network.

00:00:40.480 --> 00:00:44.559
They walk into every single interaction,

00:00:43.040 --> 00:00:47.120
whether they're picking the brain of a

00:00:44.559 --> 00:00:49.520
mentor or they're talking to a career

00:00:47.120 --> 00:00:51.360
adviser or they're trying to get a new

00:00:49.520 --> 00:00:52.879
job or they're at a networking event

00:00:51.360 --> 00:00:55.280
with other business professionals,

00:00:52.879 --> 00:00:56.640
Rotary, whatever club you're in. and

00:00:55.280 --> 00:01:00.520
they walk around thinking, "How can

00:00:56.640 --> 00:01:00.520
these people help me?"

00:01:00.640 --> 00:01:05.199
And as you can imagine, college

00:01:03.920 --> 00:01:06.799
networking events are damn near

00:01:05.199 --> 00:01:08.000
worthless because nobody can help

00:01:06.799 --> 00:01:10.400
anybody there. They don't have any

00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:12.240
skills yet. [laughter]

00:01:10.400 --> 00:01:13.280
>> So, you have a bunch of people walking

00:01:12.240 --> 00:01:14.560
around shaking each other's hands,

00:01:13.280 --> 00:01:16.560
trying to help me, but nobody has any

00:01:14.560 --> 00:01:17.759
actual skills. That's why these

00:01:16.560 --> 00:01:18.720
networking events are damn near

00:01:17.759 --> 00:01:20.000
worthless when you're in college. Should

00:01:18.720 --> 00:01:22.000
